{"step_index":129,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-18T18:15:30Z","content":"Here is the **definitive, zero-friction path** so you can develop rapidly without fighting network firewalls.\n\n---\n\n### The Definitive Fix: Free Cloudflare HTTPS Tunnel (Zero Signup, Zero Config)\n\nInstead of ngrok (which broke due to their new token policy) or local Wi-Fi routing, we use Cloudflare's free quick-tunnel. It generates a public secure `https://` URL in 5 seconds that your iPhone can load from anywhere in the world.\n\n#### Step 1: Start your Expo server on PC\nIn your project folder (`C:\\Users\\brorm\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\snap-dialer`), run:\n```cmd\nnpx expo start\n```\n\n#### Step 2: Open a second terminal window and run Cloudflare's free tunnel:\n```cmd\nnpx localtunnel --port 8081\n```\n\n#### Step 3: Connect on your iPhone\n1. The terminal will give you a public URL (e.g. `https://cool-panda-42.loca.lt`).\n2. In the **Expo Go** app on your iPhone, tap **\"Enter URL manually\"** and paste that URL (or use `exp://cool-panda-42.loca.lt`).\n3. It connects directly over public HTTPS—bypassing firewalls, routers, and local network issues 100%.\n\n---\n\n### Parallel Fast-Track: Instant Web & Mobile Testing\n\nWhile developing your core features (OCR parsing accuracy, cadence rules, and lead workflows):\n\n1. **On your PC Browser:** You can run `npx expo start --web` to test and refine all logic, table edits, and dialer flows in real time with instant hot-reloading.\n2. **On your iPhone Browser:** You can visit the same localtunnel URL in Safari on your iPhone to test camera capture and OCR live on your actual phone.\n\n---\n\n### Recommended Next Step for Rapid Development:\nRun the `npx localtunnel --port 8081` in your second terminal, or let me know if you would like me to configure a standalone cloud preview build via **EAS Build** so you have an installable app icon on your iPhone.","thinking":"**Defining the Problem Space**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the core friction points in mobile development, specifically around Windows-to-iOS networking. My focus is shifting from a general fix to a definitive solution that eliminates wasted time battling errors. I'm exploring the \"why\" behind these frustrations. My objective is to formulate a more streamlined mobile development experience.\n\n\n"}
